# Simpson's paradox: win both, lose overall
subtitle: "How a drug can win among mild cases and among severe cases yet lose overall — and why randomising the mix makes the paradox vanish."

## Simpson's paradox: win both, lose overall
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[40,100]}
vars: {"mA":50,"mB":50}

    axes ax x_label "Share of patients severe (%)" y_label "" hidden true
    curve A #b5482e expr "90 - 0.3*x" hidden true
    label l_A "drug A" above #b5482e attach_to A hidden true
    curve B #2f6b8f expr "85 - 0.3*x" hidden true
    label l_B "drug B" below #2f6b8f attach_to B hidden true
    dot pA #b5482e at.on A at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"mA"} hidden true
    dot pB #2f6b8f at.on B at.x 50 bind {"at.x":"mB"} hidden true
    path yt50 #3a3530 points [[0,50],[1.5,50]] data true hidden true
    text yl50 "50" at.data [-5,50] font_size 28 hidden true
    path yt90 #3a3530 points [[0,90],[1.5,90]] data true hidden true
    text yl90 "90" at.data [-5,90] font_size 28 hidden true
    text xmild "all mild" #8f887c at.data [7,43] font_size 26 hidden true
    text xsev "all severe" #8f887c at.data [91,43] font_size 26 hidden true
    group yticks members ["yt50","yl50","yt90","yl90","xmild","xsev","ylab"] hidden true
    path g5l #3a3530 points [[3,85],[3,90]] data true hidden true
    text t5l "+5" at.data [8,92] font_size 32 hidden true
    group gap_mild members ["g5l","t5l"] hidden true
    path g5r #3a3530 points [[97,55],[97,60]] data true hidden true
    text t5r "+5" at.data [90,56] font_size 32 hidden true
    group gap_sev members ["g5r","t5r"] hidden true
    path guA dashed #b5482e points [[80,66],[0,66]] data true hidden true
    text vA "66" #b5482e at.data [-5,66] font_size 30 hidden true
    path guB dashed #2f6b8f points [[20,79],[0,79]] data true hidden true
    text vB "79" #2f6b8f at.data [-5,79] font_size 30 hidden true
    group pooled members ["guA","vA","guB","vB"] hidden true
    path fair dashed #8f887c points [[50,44],[50,96]] data true hidden true
    text ylab "cured %" at.data [9,98] font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Simpson's paradox: win both, lose overall"

Can a drug win for mild cases and for severe cases, yet lose overall? It can, and one picture shows where the reversal comes from.
    draw ax yticks

Drug A cures ninety percent of mild cases, sixty percent of severe ones. Overall, it depends on the mix: all mild at the left, all severe at the right.
    draw A l_A

Drug B cures eighty-five and fifty-five. Its line runs five points below A's, whatever the mix.
    draw B l_B

So among mild cases, A wins by five.
    draw gap_mild

Among severe cases, A wins by five again.
    draw gap_sev

A drug's overall rate is a dot on its own line, at the share of severe patients it was given.
    draw pA

With the same mix, A's dot sits right above B's.
    draw pB

But suppose doctors saved drug A for the hardest cases: eighty percent of its patients were severe, and only twenty percent of B's.
    animate mA 80 mB 20 duration 4

Pooled, A cures sixty-six percent and B seventy-nine. B wins by thirteen, though it lost both comparisons.
    draw pooled

Neither line moved. The dots slid along them: A's pooled rate is mostly a severe-case rate, B's mostly a mild-case rate.
    highlight pA pB

    erase pooled gap_mild gap_sev

The honest comparison puts both dots on one vertical line: the same mix of patients.
    draw fair

Give both drugs half mild and half severe, and A leads by five, as it did in each group.
    animate mA 50 mB 50 duration 3

A randomised trial does this for you: chance, not a doctor, picks who gets which drug, so both get the same mix on average.
    highlight fair

The 1948 British trial of streptomycin for tuberculosis is often called the first to assign patients by random numbers.

    quiz "In a randomised trial, where would the two dots sit?"
        right: On one vertical line: chance gives both drugs the same mix of patients on average, so only the gap between the lines shows.
        + On one vertical line
        * Wherever the doctors chose
        * At the same height
---
